subproblem.natural_proof:
Source: steps 1, 3 and 6-9 of the parent-supplied proof of whitney_smooth_partition_of_unity (Simon, Introduction to GMT, Ch. 2, Theorem 1.6, proof supplied after [EG92]), extracted as a stand-alone lemma. The parent's steps 2, 4 and 5 (Vitali selection, the overlap bound (F2), local finiteness (F3)) are replaced by the hypotheses (H1)-(H4). The disjointness of the selected balls is never used directly, only (H1)-(H4).

Notation:
- ℝⁿ = EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n), with Euclidean norm |·|; U := ℝⁿ∖A.
- d(x) := Metric.infDist x A and r(x) := min{1, d(x)}/20.
- J(x) := {s ∈ J : |x − s| < 10r(s)}.
- J'(x) := {s ∈ J : ∃ z ∈ ℝⁿ, |z − s| < 10r(s) and |z − x| < r(x)}.
- B(x,ρ) and B̄(x,ρ) are the open and closed balls.
- D = fderiv ℝ, and ‖·‖ is the operator norm of a linear functional.

Hypotheses: n ∈ ℕ, J ⊆ ℝⁿ, N ∈ ℕ, and
- (H0) A ⊆ ℝⁿ is closed and nonempty;
- (H1) J ⊆ U;
- (H2) every x ∈ U has some s ∈ J with |x − s| ≤ 5r(s);
- (H3) for every x ∈ U, J(x) is finite and #J(x) ≤ N;
- (H4) for every x ∈ U, J'(x) is finite.

Claim. There exist a type ι, functions φ_i : ℝⁿ → ℝ and points p_i ∈ ℝⁿ (i ∈ ι), and reals C, M such that C ≥ 0, M ≥ 0 and:
- (P1) p_i ∈ A for every i;
- (P2) every φ_i is C¹ at every x ∈ U (ContDiffAt ℝ 1);
- (P3) φ_i(x) ≥ 0 for every i and every x ∈ U;
- (P4) every x ∈ U has a finite set F₀ ⊆ ι (the `s : Finset ι` of the Lean statement) and a neighbourhood V of x with φ_i(y) = 0 for all i ∉ F₀ and all y ∈ V;
- (P5) Σᶠ_i φ_i(x) = 1 for every x ∈ U;
- (P6) for every i and every x ∈ U, φ_i(x) ≠ 0 implies |x − p_i| ≤ C·d(x);
- (P7) for every x ∈ U with d(x) ≤ 1, Σᶠ_i ‖Dφ_i(x)‖ ≤ M/d(x).
These are the conjuncts of the Lean statement, in order.

Proof.

1. Distance and scale. Since A is closed and nonempty:
   - d(x) ≥ 0, and d(y) ≤ d(x) + |x − y| (infDist_le_infDist_add_dist);
   - x ∈ U ⇔ d(x) > 0 (IsClosed.notMem_iff_infDist_pos);
   - since ℝⁿ is proper, every y ∈ ℝⁿ has a point a ∈ A with |y − a| = d(y) (IsClosed.exists_infDist_eq_dist).
   Consequently:
   - r(x) ≥ 0 and 20r(x) ≤ d(x);
   - r(x) > 0 for x ∈ U;
   - r(x) = d(x)/20 whenever d(x) ≤ 1;
   - |r(x) − r(y)| ≤ |x − y|/20, because d is 1-Lipschitz (lipschitz_infDist_pt) and t ↦ min{1,t} is 1-Lipschitz (LipschitzWith.const_min).
   For s ∈ J, (H1) gives s ∈ U, so r_s := r(s) > 0.

2. (F1) Comparable radii. If |x − s| ≤ 10r(s), then |r(x) − r(s)| ≤ r(s)/2, so (2/3)r(x) ≤ r(s) ≤ 2r(x).

3. Inclusion. For x ∈ U, J(x) ⊆ J'(x): for s ∈ J(x) take z := x, since |x − x| = 0 < r(x).

4. (F4) Anchors. For each s ∈ J fix a_s ∈ A with |s − a_s| = d(s) (step 1). Claim: if x ∈ U, s ∈ J and |x − s| < 10r_s, then |x − a_s| < 3d(x). Indeed d(s) ≤ d(x) + |x − s|, so
   |x − a_s| ≤ |x − s| + |s − a_s| = |x − s| + d(s) ≤ d(x) + 2|x − s| < d(x) + 20r_s ≤ d(x) + 40r(x) ≤ 3d(x).
   The last two steps use r_s ≤ 2r(x) (step 2) and 20r(x) ≤ d(x).

5. Rescaled bumps. Let θ be the ContDiffBump centred at 0 ∈ ℝⁿ with rIn = 5 and rOut = 9. Then:
   - θ is C^∞ and 0 ≤ θ ≤ 1;
   - θ = 1 on B̄(0, 5);
   - its support is B(0, 9), so θ(y) = 0 when |y| ≥ 9;
   - θ is C¹ with compact support, hence Λ-Lipschitz for some Λ ≥ 0 (ContDiff.lipschitzWith_of_hasCompactSupport).
   For s ∈ J put θ_s(x) := θ((x − s)/r_s). Then:
   - θ_s is C^∞ on ℝⁿ and 0 ≤ θ_s ≤ 1;
   - θ_s(x) = 1 if |x − s| ≤ 5r_s;
   - θ_s(x) = 0 if |x − s| ≥ 9r_s, i.e. θ_s(x) ≠ 0 implies |x − s| < 9r_s;
   - θ_s is (Λ/r_s)-Lipschitz, being θ composed with the (1/r_s)-Lipschitz map x ↦ (x − s)/r_s. So ‖Dθ_s(x)‖ ≤ Λ/r_s for every x (norm_fderiv_le_of_lipschitz).
   Two vanishing properties:
   (5a) If s ∈ J and |x − s| ≥ 10r_s, then θ_s ≡ 0 on B(x, r_s), because |y − x| < r_s gives |y − s| > 9r_s. Hence Dθ_s(x) = 0.
   (5b) If x₀ ∈ U, s ∈ J ∖ J'(x₀) and y ∈ B(x₀, r(x₀)), then θ_s(y) = 0. Otherwise |y − s| < 9r_s < 10r_s and |y − x₀| < r(x₀), so z := y would show s ∈ J'(x₀).

6. Normalising sum. Let Σ(x) := Σᶠ_{s∈J} θ_s(x), the finsum over the subtype of J. Fix x₀ ∈ U and put F := J'(x₀), finite by (H4).
   - Local form. By (5b), Σ(y) = Σ_{s∈F} θ_s(y) for every y ∈ B(x₀, r(x₀)) (finsum_eq_sum_of_support_subset). This ball is a neighbourhood of x₀ because r(x₀) > 0.
   - Smoothness and derivative. Hence Σ is C^∞ at x₀ and DΣ(x₀) = Σ_{s∈F} Dθ_s(x₀) (Filter.EventuallyEq.fderiv_eq, fderiv_fun_sum). By (5a) the terms with s ∈ F ∖ J(x₀) vanish. Since J(x₀) ⊆ F by step 3, DΣ(x₀) = Σ_{s∈J(x₀)} Dθ_s(x₀).
   - Derivative bound. For s ∈ J(x₀), step 2 gives r_s ≥ (2/3)r(x₀), so ‖Dθ_s(x₀)‖ ≤ Λ/r_s ≤ (3/2)Λ/r(x₀). With #J(x₀) ≤ N from (H3), ‖DΣ(x₀)‖ ≤ (3/2)NΛ/r(x₀).
   - Lower bound. Σ(x₀) ≥ 1. By (H2) there is s₀ ∈ J with |x₀ − s₀| ≤ 5r_{s₀}, so θ_{s₀}(x₀) = 1. Moreover s₀ ∈ J(x₀) ⊆ F because 5r_{s₀} < 10r_{s₀}, and every term of the finite sum is ≥ 0.

7. The family. Let ι be the subtype {s // s ∈ J}, a Type. For s ∈ ι put φ_s(x) := θ_s(x)/Σ(x) and p_s := a_s. Let C := 3 and M := 30ΛN(N + 1). Then C ≥ 0 and M ≥ 0.

8. Verification.
   (P1) p_s = a_s ∈ A.
   (P2) Let x ∈ U. θ_s is C^∞, Σ is C^∞ at x, and Σ(x) ≥ 1 ≠ 0. So φ_s is C^∞ at x (ContDiffAt.div), in particular C¹ (ContDiffAt.of_le).
   (P3) For x ∈ U, θ_s(x) ≥ 0 and Σ(x) ≥ 1 > 0, so φ_s(x) ≥ 0.
   (P4) Let x₀ ∈ U. Let F₀ := {i ∈ ι : i ∈ J'(x₀)}, the preimage of J'(x₀) under the injective inclusion ι → ℝⁿ; it is finite by (H4) and serves as the `s : Finset ι` of the statement. Take V := B(x₀, r(x₀)) ∈ 𝓝 x₀. For i ∉ F₀ and y ∈ V, (5b) gives θ_i(y) = 0, hence φ_i(y) = 0/Σ(y) = 0.
   (P5) Let x ∈ U. By (P4) with x₀ := x and y := x, the map i ↦ φ_i(x) is supported in the finite set F₀ = {i ∈ ι : i ∈ J'(x)}. Hence Σᶠ_i φ_i(x) = (Σ_{i∈J'(x)} θ_i(x))/Σ(x) = Σ(x)/Σ(x) = 1, using step 6 with y := x and Σ(x) ≠ 0.
   (P6) Let x ∈ U with φ_s(x) ≠ 0. Then θ_s(x) ≠ 0, so |x − s| < 9r_s < 10r_s, and step 4 gives |x − p_s| < 3d(x) = C·d(x).
   (P7) Let x ∈ U with d(x) ≤ 1, so r(x) = d(x)/20.
      - If s ∉ J(x), i.e. |x − s| ≥ 10r_s, then by (5a) φ_s = θ_s/Σ vanishes on B(x, r_s), so Dφ_s(x) = 0. Hence s ↦ ‖Dφ_s(x)‖ is supported in the finite set J(x) (H3), and Σᶠ_s ‖Dφ_s(x)‖ = Σ_{s∈J(x)} ‖Dφ_s(x)‖.
      - If s ∈ J(x), the quotient rule at x (Σ(x) ≠ 0) gives Dφ_s(x) = Σ(x)⁻¹·Dθ_s(x) − θ_s(x)·Σ(x)⁻²·DΣ(x). With Σ(x) ≥ 1 and 0 ≤ θ_s(x) ≤ 1, this gives ‖Dφ_s(x)‖ ≤ ‖Dθ_s(x)‖ + ‖DΣ(x)‖. By step 6 this is ≤ (3/2)Λ/r(x) + (3/2)NΛ/r(x) = (3/2)Λ(N + 1)/r(x).
      Summing over the at most N indices of J(x):
      Σᶠ_s ‖Dφ_s(x)‖ ≤ N·(3/2)Λ(N + 1)/r(x) = 30ΛN(N + 1)/d(x) = M/d(x).

This proves (P1)-(P7). If A = ℝⁿ (in particular if n = 0), (H1) forces J = ∅ and ι is empty, so everything holds vacuously. ∎

Remarks for formalization.
(i) Bundle (H0)-(H4) into a Prop-valued structure. A Lean proof of this exact statement along the lines above was kernel-checked during decomposition. Define r, θ_s, Σ and φ_s as top-level definitions. The hypotheses as stated, with min 1 (Metric.infDist s A) / 20 written inline, are definitionally equal to their versions with r.
(ii) Useful Mathlib names:
   - ContDiffBump, with one_of_mem_closedBall, support_eq, nonneg, le_one, contDiff, hasCompactSupport;
   - ContDiff.lipschitzWith_of_hasCompactSupport, LipschitzWith.comp, norm_fderiv_le_of_lipschitz;
   - finsum_eq_sum_of_support_subset, ContDiff.sum, ContDiffAt.congr_of_eventuallyEq;
   - Filter.EventuallyEq.fderiv_eq, fderiv_fun_sum;
   - ContDiffAt.div, ContDiffAt.of_le;
   - hasDerivAt_inv, HasDerivAt.comp_hasFDerivAt, HasFDerivAt.fun_mul;
   - Finset.sum_subset, Finset.single_le_sum;
   - Set.Finite.preimage with Subtype.val_injective.injOn, Set.ncard_eq_toFinset_card, Set.ncard_image_of_injective, Subtype.image_preimage_coe;
   - IsClosed.exists_infDist_eq_dist, infDist_le_infDist_add_dist.
(iii) The pinned SimonGMT sources Poincare/SimonGMT/Chapter02/WhitneyPartition.lean and WhitneyExtension.lean implement the same construction. They use r = min{1,d}/100, bump radii 4 < 8 and near-sets {c : |x − c| ≤ r(x) + 10r(c)}. Relevant lemmas: whitneyBump_*, whitneyBumpSum_*, whitneyWeight_*, norm_fderiv_whitneyWeight_le, exists_sum_norm_fderiv_whitneyWeight_bound, WhitneyBallCover.dist_near_anchor_le. Port their source with the constants above. The finite index sets must be exactly J(x) and J'(x) of (H3) and (H4), with strict inequalities and the witness z in J'(x), not SimonGMT's near-sets. Do not import them: their prebuilt .olean files are stale for Lake.
